      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To determine after how many bounces the ball first rises to a height less than 30 cm, we follow these steps:\n\n1. **Calculate the relationship between height after each bounce:**\n\n   Let \\( h \\) represent the initial height from which the ball is dropped. The height after the first bounce can be found by multiplying the initial height by \\( \\frac{2}{3} \\):\n   \\[\n   h_1 = h \\times \\frac{2}{3} = 243 \\times \\frac{2}{3}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Perform the multiplication:**\n   \\[\n   243 \\times \\frac{2}{3} = 162 \\text{ cm}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Use the recursive formula to find subsequent heights:**\n\n   The height after each subsequent bounce can be calculated by multiplying the previous height by \\( \\frac{2}{3} \\). \n\n   The general formula for the height after the \\( n \\)-th bounce is:\n   \\[\n   h_n = 243 \\times \\left( \\frac{2}{3} \\right)^n\n   \\]\n\n4. **Determine when the height falls below 30 cm:**\n\n   We need to find the smallest positive integer \\( n \\) such that:\n   \\[\n   243 \\times \\left( \\frac{2}{3} \\right)^n < 30\n   \\]\n\n5. **Solve for \\( n \\):**\n\n   To find \\( n \\) effectively, take the logarithm on both sides of the inequality. We use logarithms with base \\( 10 \\):\n   \\[\n   \\log(243 \\times \\left( \\frac{2}{3} \\right)^n) < \\log(30)\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\log(243) + \\log\\left( \\left( \\frac{2}{3} \\right)^n \\right) < \\log(30)\n   \\]\n\n6. **Simplify the logarithmic expressions:**\n\n   Recognize that:\n   \\[\n   \\log\\left( \\left( \\frac{2}{3} \\right)^n \\right) = n \\times \\log\\left( \\frac{2}{3} \\right)\n   \\]\n\n   Also, use knowing that \\( \\log(243) = \\log(3^5) = 5 \\log(3) \\approx 5 \\times 0.477 = 2.385 \\) and similarly,\n   \\[\n   \\log(30) < \\log(32) \\approx 2 \\times 1.505 = 3.01\n   \\]\n\n    Therefore:\n    \\[\n    2.385 + n \\times \\log\\left( \\frac{2}{3} \\right) < 3.01\n    \\]\n   \\[\n   n \\times \\log\\left( \\frac{2}{3} \\right) < 3.01 - 2.385\n   \\]\n    \\[\n    n \\times \\log\\left( \\frac{2}{3} \\right) < 0.625\n    \\]\n\n   Thus:\n   \\[\n   n < \\frac{0.625}{\\log\\left( \\frac{2}{3} \\right)}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   n < \\frac{0.625}{-0.477}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   n < -1.32\n   \\]\n\n7. **Conclusion:**\n\n   Since \\( n \\) is an integer, we check smaller values to find the first \\( n \\) such that the height is below 30 cm:\n   - For \\( n = 1 \\), the height is \\( 162 \\) cm (not less than 30 cm)\n   - For \\( n = 2 \\), the height is \\( 60 \\) cm (which is less than 30 cm)\n\nThus, the ball first rises to a height less than 30 cm after \\(\\boxed{2}\\) bounces.",
